WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
THE STATE OF THE PIXEL:
As a customer, what do you want out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:07.640
of a smartphone? Affordability? Reliability?&nbsp;
Strong performance? Customizability? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:13.440
one could argue that the Google Pixel is exactly&nbsp;
that. Google prices their Pixel phones very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.080
competitively for the western market starting&nbsp;
at just $350 while offering near flagship level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:24.280
performance. Pixels also get strong reviews&nbsp;
both from users and tech journalists. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:29.240
Pixels aren’t some sort of sketchy knockoff&nbsp;
that you buy from Alibaba either. Pixels come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.320
from one of the largest and most reputable&nbsp;
companies in the world: Google. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.120
even if you don’t especially like Google, it&nbsp;
might make sense to get a Pixel and enjoy a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.760
strong smartphone experience on Google’s dime.&nbsp;
As such, you would think that this is the type of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:49.000
product that would explode in popularity. Google&nbsp;
is solving all of the issues that people generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.600
complain about when it comes to smartphones and&nbsp;
giving customers exactly what they’re asking for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:00.040
And to be fair, the Pixel is performing quite&nbsp;
well within a niche portion of the population,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:05.280
but it's very much just a niche portion&nbsp;
of the population. Globally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.360
Google doesn’t even control 1% of the market&nbsp;
despite having basically infinite resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.600
being around since the beginning of smartphones,&nbsp;
and controlling Android. That’s often pointed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.640
as one of Apple’s largest advantages. They’re&nbsp;
able to control both the software and hardware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:27.880
to create the best experience possible. But, can’t&nbsp;
Google do the exact same thing with the Pixel? I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:33.360
picking on Google here, but I’m not talking&nbsp;
about Google really at all. They’re just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.200
most notable example. You probably know a bunch&nbsp;
of other smartphone models that are even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:44.920
value packed. Maybe it’s from Xiaomi or Samsung&nbsp;
or Huawei or even the iPhone SE. But regardless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:50.200
of which value packed smartphone you’re talking&nbsp;
about, they all suffer from the same problem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:55.160
no one buys them. So, here’s why the Google&nbsp;
Pixel is having such a tough time becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.160 --> 00:02:07.580
a big player and why you should think twice&nbsp;
before giving customers exactly what they want.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:09.120
SKEPTICAL CUSTOMERS:
Taking a look back, Google’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.560
foray with smartphones date all the way back to&nbsp;
the beginning of smartphones in 2009. This year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.400
Google would launch their first ever smartphone:&nbsp;
the Android Dev Phone 1 for $400. They didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.440
market this phone very much because as the&nbsp;
name suggested it wasn’t made for consumers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.640
it was made for developers. It didn’t take much&nbsp;
longer for Google to launch a consumer oriented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:37.200
phone though. In 2010, they would come out with&nbsp;
the Nexus One but this itself would turn out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.840
a massive flop. Why you ask? Well, largely because&nbsp;
Google was going against the grain. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:49.120
locking in contracts with all of the big national&nbsp;
carriers, Google decided to sell the Nexus One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:55.400
directly to consumers for $530. Nowadays, this&nbsp;
is pretty much standard practice. Everyone knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.200
about retail pricing and contracting pricing&nbsp;
for smartphones. But, back in the late 2000s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.240
and early 2010s, there was only one pricing&nbsp;
model that the average person was familiar with:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:10.960
the contract pricing model as this was the only&nbsp;
way to buy iPhones at least within the US. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:16.440
would usually pay $100 or $200 upfront for the&nbsp;
phone and sign a 2 year contract with AT&amp;T,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:21.560
Verizon, or Sprint. From there, you would pay a&nbsp;
set amount every month for 2 years until you paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.360
off the full cost of the smartphone. The reason&nbsp;
Apple did this was to mask the true price of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:30.920
iPhones and make the price a lot more digestible&nbsp;
for a market that was seeing smartphones for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:36.360
first time. It wasn’t till mid 2011 that Apple&nbsp;
started selling unlocked iPhones for retail price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.960
on their website. And it really wasn’t till the&nbsp;
late 2010s and until T Mobile popularized unlocked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:46.720
smartphones that the average person became aware&nbsp;
of the difference between contract pricing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:53.280
retail pricing. Until then, as far as the average&nbsp;
person knew, the price of an iPhone was $200.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.400
That’s what they would have to pay upfront and&nbsp;
that’s what they saw everywhere. At stores, on TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:05.840
on magazines, on ads, everywhere. So, when Google&nbsp;
came in with an unlocked phone in 2009 for $530,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.840
the general public was naturally put off by the&nbsp;
seemingly high price. Google’s position within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.680
the overall market has changed over the years,&nbsp;
but they’re still very much going against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.520
grain. While everyone is selling phones for $800&nbsp;
or a $1000, Google is trying to sell phones for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:28.280
500 bucks, 600 bucks, but it’s the same underlying&nbsp;
issue. By giving customers exactly what they want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:33.320
you’re always gonna be going against the industry&nbsp;
at large which naturally earns you a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:39.760
skepticism. And thing is, the hardest people to&nbsp;
sell to are not people who don’t want your product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.760
but the people who don’t trust your product. If&nbsp;
you try to convince these people based on facts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:51.240
they’ll only be put off more. They’ll start&nbsp;
wondering: if your product is truly this awesome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:57.280
what’s in it for you? Whats the catch? And that’s&nbsp;
exactly what’s happening with the Google Pixel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.960
Over the years, Google has built up a reputation&nbsp;
as someone who does shady stuff to make money like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:07.520
collecting data and intruding privacy. So, when&nbsp;
Google comes out with a smartphone that seems too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.920
good to be true, people immediately wonder why.&nbsp;
And they’re not wrong for wondering why. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:17.600
it’s not like Google is handing out Pixels&nbsp;
at 0 margin as a philanthropic effort. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:21.880
they’re handing them out at 0 margin because&nbsp;
they’re ok with sacrificing profit to gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:27.400
market share and they know that they can monetize&nbsp;
your use of the Pixel using Google services. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:32.400
they do indeed have ulterior motives. And the&nbsp;
problem is that when you go against the grain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:38.160
and give customers exactly what they want, these&nbsp;
ulterior movies become more visible then ever. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:44.000
Google is constantly having to fight this uphill&nbsp;
battle against consumer skepticism. But that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:55.258
tells half the story because even consumers&nbsp;
who aren’t skeptical don’t want the Pixel.

00:05:55.258 --> 00:05:57.360
ASPIRATIONAL PURCHASE:
From the very beginning, the smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.920
industry has been somewhat of an aspirational&nbsp;
category. Half of this is because of marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:07.960
and how smartphones have been positioned over the&nbsp;
years. Apple for example has simply leaned more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:13.280
and more into premium branding especially with&nbsp;
the pro series. Realistically speaking, Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:19.600
knows that the average person won't spring for the&nbsp;
pro series. That’s why 80% of their lineup is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.520
the pro series. With that being said though, the&nbsp;
pro series is actually extremely important for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.680
the rest of Apple’s lineup because it provides&nbsp;
context. It establishes Apple as someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:35.720
puts out top tier pro level phones. And while you&nbsp;
may not be able to purchase the pro series today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:41.240
it gives you something to aspire to. This is a&nbsp;
very common marketing technique used across a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:46.800
wide range of industries. It’s the same reason&nbsp;
why Costco puts a bunch of super expensive TVs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:52.600
right at the entrance. It asserts that that’s what&nbsp;
the most successful Costco shoppers are buying.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:58.480
And by having a Costco membership, you too are&nbsp;
part of that “elite” group of shoppers. It’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:03.000
same thing with the American Express black card&nbsp;
as well. Most of these items are something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:08.880
the average person will realistically never get,&nbsp;
but the presence of these items are what make all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:14.640
the difference. So, that’s the marketing side of&nbsp;
things. The other half of the story is simply how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:20.080
smartphones fit into our lifestyles. The average&nbsp;
person spends over 3 hours on their smartphones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:25.920
everyday, and if you’re on the younger side of&nbsp;
things, the number is likely even higher. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.920
people don’t view buying smartphones the same&nbsp;
way as buying trash bags or paper towels or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:36.640
some other household item. With these items, most&nbsp;
people do just get the cheapest option with decent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.880
reliability. With smartphones, however, people&nbsp;
see it as more of an investment. They’re gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:46.720
end up spending 1000s of hours on this device,&nbsp;
so they’re much more willing to spend the extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:53.360
$100 or $200 and get the better phone even if they&nbsp;
don’t need it. And this only makes it even harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:58.320
for Google to sell the Pixel. Even if they’re able&nbsp;
to overcome the skepticism surrounding the Pixel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:03.800
consumers still just don’t want it. It’s more&nbsp;
than likely that the average consumer would say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:08.560
something along the lines of: It’s great that&nbsp;
Google is offering a well priced competitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:14.280
option, but for myself, I like getting something&nbsp;
a bit more high end. And when they say high end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:19.800
they usually just mean getting an iPhone for the&nbsp;
same price. Hearing this, you might be saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.120
that Google just needs to advertise more. If they&nbsp;
just put the Pixel infront of people more often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.400
people will finally get it right? Well, the truth&nbsp;
is that Google has already already been spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:36.280
hundreds of millions on marketing. Now to be&nbsp;
fair, that is 4-5 times less than Apple and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:42.080
Samsung historically, but here’s the deal,&nbsp;
Google’s market share is not 4-5 times less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:49.360
than Apple and Samsung. It’s more like 30-40 times&nbsp;
less. Aka, despite being the king of digital ads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:56.240
Google’s return on advertising spend is garbage in&nbsp;
comparison to Apple and Samsung. Now, this could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.040
be partially due to the the marketing campaign’s&nbsp;
themselves. Maybe, Google’s campaigns just aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:06.720
as good. But, I suspect that this has more to do&nbsp;
with all of the intangible factors that we’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:12.240
discussing. Apple and Samsung’s flagship phones&nbsp;
simply strike a chord with consumers that Google’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:20.240
budget phones do not because smartphones are often&nbsp;
an aspirational guilty pleasure type of purchase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:26.080
not something that’s strictly objective or&nbsp;
logical. And that brings us into real takeaway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:36.937
of all of this which is that consumers&nbsp;
often simply don’t know what they want.

00:09:36.937 --> 00:09:38.440
CUSTOMERS DON’T KNOW:
We often hear the business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:44.240
mantra that the customer is always right, and&nbsp;
in some industries like hospitality and tourism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:49.440
that’s a great slogan to live by. Even if the&nbsp;
customer is technically wrong, it’s better to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:55.600
assume that they’re right to keep them happy. This&nbsp;
mantra doesn’t work as well within the tech or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:01.200
really any innovation driven industry because the&nbsp;
truth is: customers have no clue what they really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:06.400
want. Henry Ford has this famous quote that&nbsp;
if he had asked customers what they wanted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.560
they would’ve said faster horses. Steve Jobs&nbsp;
very much took this quote to heart as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:16.120
strongly believed that “People don't know&nbsp;
what they want until you show it to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:21.320
That's why I never rely on market research. Our&nbsp;
task is to read things that are not yet on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:28.200
page.” And this sort of mindset is what led to&nbsp;
many of Apple’s best innovations. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:33.680
choosing to not use a stylus. Using intuitive&nbsp;
methods to zoom in and out by pinching the screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:39.520
instead of pressing a plus or minus button. Face&nbsp;
ID instead of Touch ID, and so on. Oftentimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:44.000
Apple’s products aren’t initally well received&nbsp;
by the public because consumers aren’t convinced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:49.160
that that’s what they want. The iPhone X&nbsp;
for example was memed as the $1000 emoji&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:55.400
machine. And the Airpods were scoffed at as&nbsp;
overpriced bluetooth headphones. But over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.400 --> 00:11:01.040
both of these have become extremely popular.&nbsp;
In fact, the entire world is moving towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:06.760
iOS and Android is bleeding market share. This&nbsp;
is especially true in developing countries like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:13.520
India. As soon as people are able to afford an&nbsp;
iPhone, they get an iPhone. And I’m not saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:19.600
this hype up Apple or anything. Rather, I’m trying&nbsp;
to illustrate how people don’t exactly do what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:26.600
they say. If you ask anyone, they’ll say what they&nbsp;
want is a reasonably priced reliable smartphone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:30.960
But where they actually end up spending their&nbsp;
money is an overpriced phone that fulfills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:37.640
intangible values such as status, aspirations,&nbsp;
fitting in, and whatever else. The bottomline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.320 --> 00:11:43.800
is that the Google Pixel is a manifestation of&nbsp;
what people say they want, not what they really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.600
want and that’s why Google has struggled to ever&nbsp;
grow past just a niche market. With historically&nbsp;&nbsp;

